e accepted residual"],"finding":"The ADR's core novel mechanism — a remote/time-anchored causal-stability horizon plus a forced stale-clone override — restates a well-known distributed-systems pattern (time-based vs stability-based tombstone GC, and causal-stability detection via acknowledgement/vector-clock watermarks) without an AC requiring the ADR to situate its choice against that prior art or record why the provably-safe acknowledgement-watermark alternative (the epic escalation's own option 3) was rejected.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fdf2449706b38b6a2","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria (the C1-C5 / I1-I9 proof criteria) and Scope — 'the remote-reachability computation ... for >= N days' + 'stale-clone ... FORCED remote-override protocol'","norm_id":"nca6933365717da3e","priority":0.0,"reason":"veto:absence-refuted","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A future reader or reviewer asks why rebar did not adopt a provable acknowledgement-watermark (causal-stability) scheme and finds no recorded rationale in the ADR","The accepted >N-day resurrection/data-loss residual is questioned later and the ADR cannot show the alternative was weighed"],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"In the ADR's Context/Decision, add an 'Alternatives / prior art' subsection citing the CRDT causal-stability literature (stability-based vs time-based tombstone GC; the resurrection hazard of time-based GC; acknowledgement/vector-clock watermark detection) and the epic escalation's option 3, and state the explicit rationale — no clone registry, no cross-clone clock comparison, continuous auto-push through a single remote — for choosing the remote-anchored horizon + forced stale-clone override with its accepted >N-day residual instead.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.1429,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"no","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"insufficient","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_